{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-13:14\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s “we seek help from You” is heard against failed rival invocation: 13:14 names a true call and says those called besides God cannot answer their callers.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"13:14\",\"ar\":\"لَهُۥ دَعْوَةُ ٱلْحَقِّ\"},{\"ref\":\"13:14\",\"ar\":\"لَا يَسْتَجِيبُونَ لَهُم بِشَىْءٍ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus directs its request for aid to one addressee; 13:14 contrasts that with unanswered calls to others.\",\"limits\":\"13:14 speaks of invocation and response, not the exact verb نستعين or the Fatiha’s speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-13:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s exclusive request for aid gains a practical boundary: supposed protectors besides God cannot secure even their own benefit or harm.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"13:16\",\"ar\":\"أَوْلِيَآءَ لَا يَمْلِكُونَ لِأَنفُسِهِمْ نَفْعًۭا وَلَا ضَرًّۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names its aid-source; this passage tests rival protectors by their inability to help themselves.\",\"limits\":\"The passage refutes these protectors’ power; it does not detail every kind of help people may give one another.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:35\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim asks his Lord to keep him and his sons from worshipping idols, making protection of worship itself a need brought to God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:35\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱجْعَلْ هَٰذَا ٱلْبَلَدَ ءَامِنًۭا وَٱجْنُبْنِى وَبَنِىَّ أَن نَّعْبُدَ ٱلْأَصْنَامَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:36\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ إِنَّهُنَّ أَضْلَلْنَ كَثِيرًۭا مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins worship to seeking help; Ibrahim’s prayer makes avoidance of rival worship one concrete object of divine aid.\",\"limits\":\"Ibrahim names his family and idols, while the focus leaves the particular help sought unstated.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:49\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The root shared by نَعْبُدُ and عِبَادِي places worshippers beside the identity of servants whom God tells of His forgiveness and mercy.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:49\",\"ar\":\"نَبِّئْ عِبَادِىٓ أَنِّىٓ أَنَا ٱلْغَفُورُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s first-person worship verb activates the same ع ب د family used for God’s servants.\",\"limits\":\"The noun عِبَادِي does not identify the Fatiha’s speakers specifically, and this verse does not mention their request for aid.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:87\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"If the seven oft-repeated passages are the Fatiha, then this ayah frames 1:5 as part of a recitation whose repeated form is itself named.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:87\",\"ar\":\"سَبْعًۭا مِّنَ ٱلْمَثَانِى\"}],\"missing\":\"This text does not identify the seven as the Fatiha; an explicit identification of the seven oft-repeated passages, or other evidence linking them to this seven-ayah surah, could establish the connection.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:98\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After the Prophet’s distress, the passage turns to praise, prostration, and worship that continues until certainty; it presents worship as a response sustained through strain.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:97\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ نَعْلَمُ أَنَّكَ يَضِيقُ صَدْرُكَ بِمَا يَقُولُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:98\",\"ar\":\"فَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ وَكُن مِّنَ ٱلسَّٰجِدِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:99\",\"ar\":\"وَٱعْبُدْ رَبَّكَ حَتَّىٰ يَأْتِيَكَ ٱلْيَقِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus couples worship with dependence; the neighboring sequence makes worship a continuing practice amid distress.\",\"limits\":\"The passage commands the singular addressee to worship and does not explicitly connect that worship to seeking aid.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s declaration can be heard as the communal answer to the prophetic summons to worship God and avoid false powers.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:36\",\"ar\":\"أَنِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱجْتَنِبُوا۟ ٱلطَّٰغُوتَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages make worship’s object decisive, and 16:36 explicitly pairs worship with avoiding a rival.\",\"limits\":\"The earlier verse reports a command to every community; it does not quote the Fatiha’s first-person plural response.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:51\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The emphatic object in فَإِيَّايَ parallels the focus’s repeated إِيَّاكَ: both place the divine addressee before a verb and intensify exclusive orientation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:51\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّمَا هُوَ إِلَٰهٌۭ وَٰحِدٌۭ ۖ فَإِيَّٰىَ فَٱرْهَبُونِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus repeats the fronted pronoun across worship and aid; 16:51 uses a related emphatic pronoun after affirming one deity.\",\"limits\":\"The command there is to fear God, not to worship or seek aid, and the pronoun forms differ in person and case.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:53\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s unqualified aid-seeking is tested by a sequence in which people cry to God in distress, then some associate partners after relief.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:53\",\"ar\":\"فَإِلَيْهِ تَجْـَٔرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:54\",\"ar\":\"إِذَا فَرِيقٌۭ مِّنكُم بِرَبِّهِمْ يُشْرِكُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins worship and aid in a present-tense communal declaration; these verses ask whether exclusive turning lasts after hardship passes.\",\"limits\":\"The passage describes some people’s conduct in a particular sequence; it does not say the focus’s speakers behave this way.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:73\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage contrasts worship of beings unable to provide sustenance with the focus’s seeking aid from its chosen addressee.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:73\",\"ar\":\"وَيَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ مَا لَا يَمْلِكُ لَهُمْ رِزْقًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus directs dependence to God; 16:73 tests rival objects by their inability to provide even sustenance.\",\"limits\":\"Sustenance is one kind of provision, not a full definition of the aid sought in 1:5.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:114\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"إِيَّاهُ تَعْبُدُونَ repeats the focus’s fronted emphatic object before the worship verb, while placing that worship in a context of provision and gratitude.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:114\",\"ar\":\"وَٱشْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ إِن كُنتُمْ إِيَّاهُ تَعْبُدُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared object-pronoun-plus-worship-verb construction directly echoes the focus’s first clause.\",\"limits\":\"16:114 links worship to gratitude for food and blessing; it does not include a request for aid.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The prohibition on taking a trustee besides God gives the focus’s aid-seeking a governance dimension: the ultimate one entrusted with affairs is God alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:2\",\"ar\":\"أَلَّا تَتَّخِذُوا۟ مِن دُونِى وَكِيلًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus addresses God as its aid-source; 17:2 excludes a wakīl taken besides Him.\",\"limits\":\"A wakīl is not simply every person who offers practical help, and 17:2 does not use the verb نستعين.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:31\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:42\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The hypothetical rival gods would themselves seek a way to the Lord of the Throne, casting even supposed deities as seekers beneath a higher authority.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:42\",\"ar\":\"لَّٱبْتَغَوْا۟ إِلَىٰ ذِى ٱلْعَرْشِ سَبِيلًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus speaks as a dependent plural seeking help; 17:42 imagines rival powers seeking access upward.\",\"limits\":\"The hypothetical concerns a path to the Throne’s Lord, not the exact request for aid in 1:5.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:56\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Those invoked besides God cannot remove harm, and the next verse portrays the invoked beings themselves as seeking nearness to their Lord; they are dependents, not final sources of aid.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:56\",\"ar\":\"فَلَا يَمْلِكُونَ كَشْفَ ٱلضُّرِّ عَنكُمْ وَلَا تَحْوِيلًا\"},{\"ref\":\"17:57\",\"ar\":\"أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ يَدْعُونَ يَبْتَغُونَ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِمُ ٱلْوَسِيلَةَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus directs aid-seeking to God; the adjacent scene shows the claimed intermediaries needing a way to their own Lord.\",\"limits\":\"The invoked beings in this scene are not shown to represent every possible object of worship or every kind of intercession.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:65\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God’s servants are protected from Satan’s authority, and the Lord is named as a sufficient trustee; this joins servant identity with divine sufficiency.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:65\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ عِبَادِى لَيْسَ لَكَ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطَٰنٌۭ ۚ وَكَفَىٰ بِرَبِّكَ وَكِيلًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s worship and aid clauses voice a servant’s dependence, while 17:65 names servants and God as trustee.\",\"limits\":\"17:65 addresses Satan’s lack of dominion; it does not define the full range of the focus’s aid request.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:14\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The youths’ first-person plural pledge not to invoke any deity besides their Lord reads like a pressured narrative counterpart to the focus’s communal declaration of worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:14\",\"ar\":\"رَبُّنَا رَبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ لَن نَّدْعُوَا۟ مِن دُونِهِۦٓ إِلَٰهًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both are plural acts of commitment to one Lord; the youths state theirs as a refusal of rival invocation.\",\"limits\":\"Their wording concerns invocation and refusal of other gods, not an explicit request for help.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The youths withdraw from their people and their worship, take refuge in the cave, and are promised their Lord’s mercy; exclusive orientation appears as an enacted refuge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:16\",\"ar\":\"فَأْوُۥٓا۟ إِلَى ٱلْكَهْفِ يَنشُرْ لَكُمْ رَبُّكُم مِّن رَّحْمَتِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus links worship and dependence; the episode presents withdrawal from rival worship alongside trust in the Lord’s mercy.\",\"limits\":\"The cave is their immediate physical refuge, and the verse does not say they utter the Fatiha’s formula.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The cave account locates guardianship and judgment solely with God, extending the focus’s exclusive address from worship and aid to authority.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:26\",\"ar\":\"مَا لَهُم مِّن دُونِهِۦ مِن وَلِىٍّۢ وَلَا يُشْرِكُ فِى حُكْمِهِۦٓ أَحَدًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus addresses one source of aid; this passage denies any guardian besides Him and any partner in His judgment.\",\"limits\":\"The verse concerns guardianship and judgment in a specific story, not the details of worship or prayer.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:65\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The noun عَبْدًا shares the ع ب د root with the focus’s نَعْبُدُ, but here it names a servant rather than the act of worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:65\",\"ar\":\"فَوَجَدَا عَبْدًۭا مِّنْ عِبَادِنَآ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared root offers a lexical point of contact between worship and servant status.\",\"limits\":\"This encounter does not connect that servant to the Fatiha’s speakers or their aid-seeking.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:95\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Dhu’l-Qarnayn says his Lord’s enabling is better, then asks people to assist with strength; the focus’s exclusive aid-address can coexist with practical human cooperation under divine enablement.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:95\",\"ar\":\"مَا مَكَّنِّى فِيهِ رَبِّى خَيْرٌۭ فَأَعِينُونِى بِقُوَّةٍ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses Form X نستعين for aid-seeking; 18:95 uses a related ع و ن verb for a human request and explicitly ranks the Lord’s enabling as better.\",\"limits\":\"This scene does not explain the grammar of إياك or establish a general rule for all human assistance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:102\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The condemned arrangement is taking God’s servants as protectors besides Him, a pointed test of whether divine servants can displace the focus’s exclusive aid-source.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:102\",\"ar\":\"أَن يَتَّخِذُوا۟ عِبَادِى مِن دُونِىٓ أَوْلِيَآءَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus directs dependence to God; 18:102 names servants of God being treated as protectors instead.\",\"limits\":\"The verse condemns taking them as awliya; it does not prohibit every practical request for help from another person.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-19:2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The next verse shows Zakariyya calling privately on his Lord, giving the focus’s aid-seeking a concrete prophetic scene of personal appeal within servant status.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"19:2\",\"ar\":\"ذِكْرُ رَحْمَتِ رَبِّكَ عَبْدَهُۥ زَكَرِيَّآ\"},{\"ref\":\"19:3\",\"ar\":\"إِذْ نَادَىٰ رَبَّهُۥ نِدَآءً خَفِيًّۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s plural request for aid is activated by the adjacent account of one servant calling on his Lord.\",\"limits\":\"The request’s contents and outcome are not included in these two verses, and Zakariyya’s call is singular and private.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-19:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The statement that God is both my Lord and your Lord leads directly to worship and names this as a straight path, echoing the focus’s worship before the Fatiha asks for guidance along the straight path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"19:36\",\"ar\":\"فَٱعْبُدُوهُ ۚ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌۭ مُّسْتَقِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s worship clause immediately precedes its request for the straight path; 19:36 explicitly joins worship to that path.\",\"limits\":\"19:36 gives a command and description, not the Fatiha’s first-person petition for guidance.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-19:65\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Worship is paired with steadfast endurance in worship, giving the focus’s present-tense declaration a frame of sustained practice.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"19:65\",\"ar\":\"فَٱعْبُدْهُ وَٱصْطَبِرْ لِعِبَٰدَتِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared worship root connects the focus’s declaration with an injunction to persist in worship.\",\"limits\":\"The command addresses the Prophet singularly and adds endurance, which the focus does not state explicitly.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-19:91\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-20:14\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The divine self-identification and command to worship Me give a direct singular counterpart to the focus’s plural You-only worship declaration.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"20:14\",\"ar\":\"لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّآ أَنَا۠ فَٱعْبُدْنِى وَأَقِمِ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ لِذِكْرِىٓ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages pair exclusive deity with worship directed to God; 20:14 adds prayer for remembrance.\",\"limits\":\"20:14 is a singular command in Moses’ encounter and does not mention seeking help.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-20:33\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Moses and Aaron seek partnership so that they may praise God abundantly, placing collective divine-directed devotion after a request for human assistance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"20:32\",\"ar\":\"وَأَشْرِكْهُ فِىٓ أَمْرِى\"},{\"ref\":\"20:33\",\"ar\":\"كَىْ نُسَبِّحَكَ كَثِيرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s plural worship has a scene parallel in two speakers naming joint praise as their aim.\",\"limits\":\"This passage asks a human to share a mission, not God for aid, and tasbīḥ is not the verb نعبد.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-20:34\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The same shared mission names abundant remembrance of God as its purpose, adding remembrance as one form of the focus’s collective devotion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"20:34\",\"ar\":\"وَنَذْكُرَكَ كَثِيرًا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both use a first-person plural action directed toward God, with Moses and Aaron stating remembrance as a joint aim.\",\"limits\":\"Remembrance is not an explicit topic in 1:5, and this verse does not ask God for help.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-21:25\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus can be heard as the first-person enactment of a message attributed to every earlier messenger: no deity except God, therefore worship Him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"21:25\",\"ar\":\"أَنَّهُۥ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّآ أَنَا۠ فَٱعْبُدُونِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus voices exclusive worship; this verse presents that orientation as a repeated prophetic message.\",\"limits\":\"The universal messenger formula does not include the focus’s aid-seeking clause.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-21:112\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The rare direct pairing of a plea to the Lord with الرَّحْمَٰنُ الْمُسْتَعَانُ gives an exact aid-root counterpart to نستعين and names the Merciful as the one whose aid is sought.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"21:112\",\"ar\":\"قَٰلَ رَبِّ ٱحْكُم بِٱلْحَقِّ\"},{\"ref\":\"21:112\",\"ar\":\"وَرَبُّنَا ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ ٱلْمُسْتَعَانُ عَلَىٰ مَا تَصِفُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages direct dependence to the Lord; الْمُسْتَعَانُ is a Form X aid-word cognate with نستعين.\",\"limits\":\"The plea asks for judgment in a particular dispute, whereas the Fatiha leaves the aid requested open.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-22:10\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The following verse distinguishes steady devotion from worship that lasts only while fortune is good, cautioning against hearing the focus’s joined worship and aid as a bargain for comfort.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"22:10\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَيْسَ بِظَلَّٰمٍۢ لِّلْعَبِيدِ\"},{\"ref\":\"22:11\",\"ar\":\"يَعْبُدُ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ حَرْفٍۢ ۖ فَإِنْ أَصَابَهُۥ خَيْرٌ ٱطْمَأَنَّ بِهِۦ ۖ وَإِنْ أَصَابَتْهُ فِتْنَةٌ ٱنقَلَبَ عَلَىٰ وَجْهِهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus couples worship with a request for help; the adjacent example exposes devotion that turns when tested.\",\"limits\":\"The passage describes a particular unstable worshipper and does not imply that seeking help itself is conditional worship.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-22:62\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The contrast between Allah as the Real and what is invoked besides Him as false sharpens the focus’s exclusive address as a choice of the true addressee.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"22:62\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنَّ مَا يَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِهِۦ هُوَ ٱلْبَٰطِلُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus front-loads its chosen addressee; 22:62 explicitly contrasts Him with those invoked besides Him.\",\"limits\":\"This verse supplies a truth-versus-falsehood contrast without naming worship or aid-seeking.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-22:77\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The adjacent call to hold fast to God and its naming Him as the excellent patron and helper make worship and dependence a paired communal practice.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"22:77\",\"ar\":\"وَٱعْبُدُوا۟ رَبَّكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"22:78\",\"ar\":\"وَٱعْتَصِمُوا۟ بِٱللَّهِ هُوَ مَوْلَىٰكُمْ ۖ فَنِعْمَ ٱلْمَوْلَىٰ وَنِعْمَ ٱلنَّصِيرُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus joins worship to seeking aid; 22:77–78 place worship beside holding fast to God as patron and helper.\",\"limits\":\"The later verse calls God helper and patron but does not use the Fatiha’s request form.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:23\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Noah’s public call to worship Allah because there is no other deity supplies the prophetic imperative to which the focus’s plural declaration can answer.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:23\",\"ar\":\"يَٰقَوْمِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مَا لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرُهُۥٓ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus affirms worship of one addressee; Noah states the same boundary as a summons to his people.\",\"limits\":\"Noah’s verse is an imperative within a prophetic story, not the Fatiha’s first-person prayer.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"A later messenger repeats Noah’s exclusive-worship summons for another generation, making the focus’s confession resonate with a transgenerational call.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:31\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ أَنشَأْنَا مِنۢ بَعْدِهِمْ قَرْنًا ءَاخَرِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:32\",\"ar\":\"أَنِ ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مَا لَكُم مِّنْ إِلَٰهٍ غَيْرُهُۥٓ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated formula follows the emergence of another generation, while the focus voices the shared confession from within its worshipping community.\",\"limits\":\"This episode does not quote the Fatiha or describe its speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:1\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s worship verb and the description of the messenger as God’s servant share the ع ب د root, connecting communal worship with the messenger’s servant identity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:1\",\"ar\":\"عَلَىٰ عَبْدِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same root appears as an act in the focus and a status in the messenger’s commission.\",\"limits\":\"The verse concerns revelation and warning, not the Fatiha’s aid request or the worshippers’ identity.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:4\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The root ع و ن also appears in a hostile allegation that other people helped the Prophet; the shared root alone does not make that alleged assistance devotional.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:4\",\"ar\":\"وَأَعَانَهُۥ عَلَيْهِ قَوْمٌ ءَاخَرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s aid verb and the accusation’s assistance verb share a root while taking different forms and objects.\",\"limits\":\"The passage reports an accusation, not established assistance, and gives no direct account of the focus’s request.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"A person can make desire itself a deity; the focus’s exclusive address therefore reaches beyond named idols to an inward rival orientation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:43\",\"ar\":\"ٱتَّخَذَ إِلَٰهَهُۥ هَوَىٰهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus assigns worship to one addressee, while this verse calls desire a deity taken by a person.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not name desire or spell out a psychological application of its exclusivity.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:55\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Worship directed elsewhere is exposed as dependence on what neither benefits nor harms, a practical counterexample to the focus’s chosen source of aid.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:55\",\"ar\":\"وَيَعْبُدُونَ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ مَا لَا يَنفَعُهُمْ وَلَا يَضُرُّهُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus directs worship and aid to one addressee; 25:55 joins rival worship to incapacity for benefit or harm.\",\"limits\":\"The passage criticizes a rival cult and does not define all forms of human dependence.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:22\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The root of نَعْبُدُ also appears in Pharaoh’s charge عَبَّدتَّ, meaning that Moses enslaved the Israelites; its causative form brings coercive servitude into tension with the focus’s worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:22\",\"ar\":\"أَنْ عَبَّدتَّ بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ع ب د root links the act of worship to a scene of forced subjection, while the forms and agents differ.\",\"limits\":\"Pharaoh’s accusation is not the focus’s meaning, and this verse does not establish that the two forms are interchangeable.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:70\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim’s question makes the object of worship the issue; the focus can be heard as answering that question in advance with You, before naming the act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:70\",\"ar\":\"مَا تَعْبُدُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:71\",\"ar\":\"نَعْبُدُ أَصْنَامًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus leads with its worship-object, while Ibrahim’s question asks what his people worship and the next verse names idols.\",\"limits\":\"The interrogative word order in Ibrahim’s question is not the same grammatical construction as the focus’s fronted emphatic pronoun.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:71\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The people’s answer, “we worship idols and remain devoted to them,” is a direct rival first-person plural to the focus’s “we worship You,” and adds sustained attachment to the rival object.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:71\",\"ar\":\"نَعْبُدُ أَصْنَامًۭا فَنَظَلُّ لَهَا عَٰكِفِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both utterances use first-person plural worship, but one front-loads God and the other names idols before describing continued devotion to them.\",\"limits\":\"The scene contrasts idol worship with the focus’s orientation; it does not describe the Fatiha’s recitation setting.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:91\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The speaker says he is commanded only to worship the Lord of the town, whose is everything; this gives the focus’s exclusive worship a public prophetic formulation grounded in ownership.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:91\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّمَآ أُمِرْتُ أَنْ أَعْبُدَ رَبَّ هَٰذِهِ ٱلْبَلْدَةِ ٱلَّذِى حَرَّمَهَا وَلَهُۥ كُلُّ شَىْءٍ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus declares worship for one addressee; the speaker identifies the Lord of the town as the sole object of his command.\",\"limits\":\"This is the singular messenger’s commission and does not state the focus’s aid request.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:70\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Exclusive deity, praise in both lives, and sole judgment form the authority frame within which the focus’s worship and aid-seeking are directed.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"28:70\",\"ar\":\"لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ ۖ لَهُ ٱلْحَمْدُ فِى ٱلْأُولَىٰ وَٱلْءَاخِرَةِ ۖ وَلَهُ ٱلْحُكْمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s exclusive address is paired here with exclusive deity and judgment.\",\"limits\":\"28:70 does not itself command worship or ask for help.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim’s call to worship God and be mindful of Him is followed by a call to seek provision from God, joining devotion to dependence in the same scene.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:16\",\"ar\":\"ٱعْبُدُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَٱتَّقُوهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:17\",\"ar\":\"فَٱبْتَغُوا۟ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرِّزْقَ وَٱعْبُدُوهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s two clauses join worship and aid; Ibrahim’s appeal develops into seeking provision from God and worshipping Him.\",\"limits\":\"Provision is a particular need, and Ibrahim addresses his people rather than reciting a first-person prayer.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:17\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage argues from rival worshippers’ inability to provide sustenance to a direct command to seek provision from God and worship Him, an explicit pairing of dependence and devotion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:17\",\"ar\":\"لَا يَمْلِكُونَ لَكُمْ رِزْقًۭا فَٱبْتَغُوا۟ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرِّزْقَ وَٱعْبُدُوهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names one addressee for worship and aid; 29:17 makes the same dependency contrast explicit through provision.\",\"limits\":\"The verse specifies sustenance and does not equate every kind of aid with provision.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
